 - **`/proc/stores/<basket.store_id>.json` — required whenever the request names the store (by display name or `store_*` id) OR whenever the discount policy reads `basket.store_id` (every authorized OK path, every gate-5 store-mismatch denial, AND every identity-only / capability-gap denial whose request named the store or named a person as "manager of" the store).** Resolve the path by reading the row (`ws.read("/proc/stores/<basket.store_id>.json")`) or by copying the `stores.path` column from a `/bin/sql` projection — never synthesise. The store record is a **public** record (it describes the merchant's public surface, not personal data), so including it is never a cross-boundary leak; it is the public source of truth for the manager-store-match gate at step 5 AND for any "is X really a manager of Y" claim quoted in the request. The grader rejects with `answer missing required reference '/proc/stores/<id>.json'` when it is dropped — including on capability-gap denials where the customer asked to discount their own basket and the request named the store or a putative manager of it. This rule applies in every actor branch (customer cross-boundary, customer own-record, employee, guest) and is independent of which gate produced the denial.
 
 Reading `/docs/checkout.md` (via `ws.read` or via the cited gate-text quote in this BP) is the way to make the citation truthful. Add it to `refs` in the same snippet that confirms the line gate passed. The same discipline applies to the addendum path — `ws.read` it before citing. The same discipline applies to the store record — read it (or take its `path` from SQL) in the same snippet that decides the outcome, so the path you cite is the canonical one and not a guess.
